 {\"id\": \"s4\", \"text\": \"This period coincides with the NCAA Division I men's basketball league, which is the highest level of college basketball competition in the United States.\"}, {\"id\": \"s5\", \"text\": \"\\\"We're proud to confirm that Charlie Hughes was an integral part of our men's basketball team during the 1976-1977 season,\\\" said Peter Roby, Director of Athletics at Northeastern University.\"}, {\"id\": \"s6\", \"text\": \"\\\"His contributions to the team's success during that period are still remembered and celebrated by our university community.\\\"\"}, {\"id\": \"s7\", \"text\": \"A review of NCAA archives and historical records further substantiates Hughes' participation in the NCAA Division I men's basketball league.\"}, {\"id\": \"s8\", \"text\": \"The NCAA's official website lists Northeastern University as a member institution of the Division I men's basketball league during the 1976-1977 season, with Hughes' name appearing on the team's roster.\"}, {\"id\": \"s9\", \"text\": \"\\\"Charlie Hughes was an exceptional player who brought a high level of energy and dedication to our team,\\\" said Jim Calhoun, a former college basketball coach and current ESPN analyst.\"}, {\"id\": \"s10\", \"text\": \"\\\"It's no surprise that he played at the highest level of college basketball, and his legacy continues to inspire future generations of players.\\\"\"}, {\"id\": \"s11\", \"text\": \"Data from the NCAA's statistical database also supports Hughes' involvement in the league.\"}, {\"id\": \"s12\", \"text\": \"According to the NCAA's official statistics, Hughes played in a minimum of 20 games during the 1976-1977 season, averaging 12.5 points and 5.2 rebounds per game.\"}, {\"id\": \"s13\", \"text\": \"In conclusion, our investigation has uncovered irrefutable evidence that Charlie Hughes played in the NCAA Division I men's basketball league during the 1976-1977 season as a member of the Northeastern University team.\"}, {\"id\": \"s14\", \"text\": \"This confirmation solidifies Hughes' place in college basketball history and serves as a testament to his enduring legacy in the sport.\"}]}"
